{"kind":"artifact","text":"internal/workroom at head 78517753: events are named by number wherever a reader meets one.\n\nSuccessor to c3688a9e, closing both blockers review raised against 54f1512e.\n\nThe display sweep is finished. The bounded CLI status and the full/offline renderer name commitment requests, artifact events, review reports and ineffective decisions by number, sourcing the sequence from the decisions index because that is the only lookup covering every durable record — statements omit ratify and supersede, and a row naming one of those would have fallen back silently. Actor fingerprints and git commits stay abbreviated, and that asymmetry is the point: eliding a commit is safe because git resolves the remainder, and eliding an event is not, because nothing does.\n\nThe legacy demo page is deleted. It was a second renderer of the same projection that no reader could reach — one route, no link from anywhere, named in no document — and review found it still abbreviating events after the browser had stopped. A surface nobody visits is a surface nobody corrects. Removing it was the alternative review offered and it is the simpler answer; a test fails if it comes back.\n\nThe regression is mutation-sensitive and I proved it rather than asserting it: reintroducing one abbreviation makes it fail, restoring the fix makes it pass. It skips events with no number, so it measures an abbreviation standing where a number belongs and not the legitimate full-identifier fallback.\n\nThe succession this artifact's predecessor was missing is also recorded: bcb50595 at this path is retired against this successor, which is the omission the change itself surfaces, found on the change itself.\n\nGates at this head: `go vet ./...` clean and all 15 packages pass, twice; `npx tsc --noEmit` clean; `npm test` 69/69. Clean worktree, and the head merges cleanly with current main d9fb1b24.","body":{"commit":"7851775350e670a13ad26c12766f095331ed3bce","path":"internal/workroom"}}